engine\kernel\API.md-590-| Member | Meaning |
engine\kernel\API.md-591-|---|---|
engine\kernel\API.md-592-| `TLHand.create(ctx, profile = "regular", overrides = {}) -> TLHand` | a hand; `overrides` replaces profile numbers (keys of the table below) and takes `stream` (an int: a second hand in the same run draws its own numbers); an unknown profile or key is an error |
engine\kernel\API.md:593:| `TLHand.PROFILES`, `TLHand.profiles() -> PackedStringArray`, `TLHand.ON`, `TLHand.OFF` | the profiles as data; a wished strength of at least `ON` (0.35) presses a key, which stays wished until the strength falls below `OFF` (0.15) |
engine\kernel\API.md-594-| `profile: String`, `params: Dictionary`, `is_perfect() -> bool` | which hand, its numbers as used |
engine\kernel\API.md-595-| `press(action, value = 1.0)`, `release(action)`, `tap(action, frames = 1)`, `set_axis(negative, positive, value)`, `release_all()` | intents, named as `ctx.input`'s injection |
engine\kernel\API.md-596-| `steer(left, right, up, down, direction: Vector2)` | a direction (x right, y down) on four keys: quantised to 8 ways, a turn lets go of the old key late |
engine\kernel\API.md-597-| `set_aim(action, point: Vector3)`, `clear_aim(action)` | **K4: the aim through a person's hand.** Where the bot wants to point an aim (a declared `ctx.input.declare_aim`); call it every tick it points (a moving target: every tick). `perfect`: straight to `ctx.input.set_aim` on the same tick (a ported bot keeps its hash: `ex_pointer`). A person's profile moves the game's aim as a cursor: a target that jumped more than `aim_jump` (1 m) is followed only `aim_lag` frames later (the cursor stays meanwhile); then the cursor travels at most `aim_speed` m/s on the ground and slows near its goal (it closes `aim_settle` of the rest each second); the goal is the target plus a miss drawn per target (up to `aim_miss` m, any side); a target moving on without jumping is tracked with no new reaction; during a lapse the cursor holds still. `clear_aim` takes the cursor away at the hand's next look. A mouse button needs nothing: it is a key of a declared action (`declare(&"shoot", [KEY_SPACE], [MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT])`) and the hand presses the action |
engine\kernel\API.md-598-| `tick()` | the last line of `_bot_tick`: the hand sees this frame's intents (unless it is away) and presses or releases what is due |
engine\kernel\API.md-599-| `stats() -> Dictionary` | the proof that the hand ran, for `_bot_result()`: `decisions` (reactions), `mean_reaction_frames`, `stops` (feed-forward releases), `presses`, `releases`, `taps`, `lengthened` (a release postponed to a person's tap length), `sticks` (held through a change), `held_late_frames`, `overshoots`, `lapses`, `lapse_frames`, `intents`, `aims`, `profile`; K4: `taps_lost` (taps the bot asked for while the hand was away in a lapse: never pressed), `aim_targets` (new targets the cursor reacted to), `aim_lag_frames`, `mean_aim_lag_frames` |
engine\kernel\API.md-600-
--
engine\kernel\API.md-608-- **keys, not an analog axis.** `set_axis(-1..1)` becomes one key down or none (`ON` / `OFF`), `steer` one of 8 ways.
engine\kernel\API.md-609-- **human lengths.** A press lasts at least a `tap` (4 to 9 frames). A stop is let go `release_late` frames late; a
engine\kernel\API.md-610-  turn (one key of a pair or of W A S D goes down while another goes up) lets go of the old key `overshoot` frames
engine\kernel\API.md-611-  late instead, and the opposite key of a pair (left and right, up and down) goes down only once the old one is up:
engine\kernel\API.md-612-  a person does not hold both. With `stick_chance` a key stays down `stick` frames more: the button still held when
engine\kernel\API.md-613-  the situation changed.
engine\kernel\API.md-614-- **attention.** `lapses_per_minute` times a minute on average the hand is away for `lapse` frames: intents made then
engine\kernel\API.md:615:  are not seen (a tap is lost; a held wish is seen when it comes back).
